WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.420
Anyone who's going to spend the
time and effort to learn a language

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.990
should understand how the process
works and there is no better

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.700
explainer than Stephen krashen.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.710
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.090
Today, I'm going to talk about
Stephen Krashen and the theory

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.980
of language acquisition.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.820
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.770
on the bell for notific...

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.590
notifications.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:28.710
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.710
I appreciate it.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:35.955
So, uh, I mentioned Steven Krashen in one
of my previous videos, and I said that

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:43.095
he has put out a book, a thin book, uh,
which really, he says all there is that

00:00:43.095 --> 00:00:47.125
we need to know about language acquisition
and someone asked, what is the book?

00:00:47.475 --> 00:00:51.375
Well, actually there are quite a few books
and I would recommend that people Google

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:55.785
for Stephen Krashen go to his website and
you'll find all kinds of free material.

00:00:56.235 --> 00:01:00.675
And I think it's important for people
who are interested to learn a language,

00:01:00.705 --> 00:01:05.384
interested in language learning, that they
understand how the process works and the

00:01:05.384 --> 00:01:10.925
best explainer of how we learn languages
in my opinion, by far, is Stephen Krashen.

00:01:11.325 --> 00:01:15.615
And, uh, so I was gonna, I thought
I would do one or several videos

00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:20.535
where I walk through some of his
explanations about language learning.

00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:25.455
So this is a book that I got
in Taipei when I was there

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.405
I don't know, 15 years ago.

00:01:27.615 --> 00:01:32.055
And he was presenting there and I didn't
know him, but I listened to him and

00:01:32.055 --> 00:01:33.915
it was kind of like a Eureka moment.

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:34.965
And I bought this book.

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:40.755
So let's just start here and it's not like
I'm reading from the Bible, but almost.

00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:43.485
So the principles of language acquisition.

00:01:44.265 --> 00:01:48.855
First of all the following five
hypotheses, you know, explanations of

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:53.595
how we learn are the core of current
theory on language acquisition.

00:01:53.625 --> 00:01:58.065
First of all, the acquisition
learning hypothesis claims that

00:01:58.065 --> 00:02:02.205
we have two independent ways
of developing language ability.

00:02:02.685 --> 00:02:07.365
One is language acquisition as
a subconscious process and the

00:02:07.365 --> 00:02:11.045
second one is a deliberate effort,
the way we learn in school.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:21.045
And his point is that we learn primarily
naturally in a, as a subconscious process

00:02:21.795 --> 00:02:27.405
and that the rules and the grammar that we
learn at school don't help us very much.

00:02:28.695 --> 00:02:34.005
The second hypothesis is the natural order
hypothesis, which states that there is

00:02:34.005 --> 00:02:39.540
a natural or.der with which we're going
to acquire the elements of a language.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.450
And there's a natural order, uh, for
acquiring certain elements of grammar.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.270
And it doesn't necessarily relate
to how simple or complex those

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.310
particular grammar issues are.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.709
And he uses the example of the sort
of progressive form in English.

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:02.880
The "...ing" I'm going, learning,
doing versus the third person singular,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.799
which takes an "s" as we know.

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:07.140
Takes an "s" takes an "s".

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:13.549
So even though the idea that the third
person singular in the present tense takes

00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:19.799
an "s" is a very simple concept, it takes
a long time for most language learners,

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.630
people learning English to acquire.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.410
Whereas the "...ing" form, that
continuous or the progressive

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.930
form, is acquired very early.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.120
And studies have shown that a number of
issues in grammar in different languages

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.960
seem to be acquired in the same order
and it doesn't really matter how much

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.940
effort is put into teaching these.

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.240
It's not because something
is taught that we acquire it

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.460
because there is a natural order.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.940
And so the natural order is not
based on simplicity or complexity,

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.940
it's immune to deliberate teaching.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:03.195
And so the, and the correlary in a
way is that, you know, the natural

00:04:03.195 --> 00:04:05.115
order is not the teaching order.

00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:09.615
So you may still teach different
things, but it's going to be acquired

00:04:09.675 --> 00:04:11.505
according to this natural order.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.550
The third element of his
language acquisition theory is

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:23.040
the monitor hypothesis and the
conscious sort of teaching of the

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.710
language  creates in us a monitor.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.680
Here are the correct rules.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.715
Here is how the language is spoken,
but he makes the point that the

00:04:31.715 --> 00:04:37.355
monitor, our knowledge of grammar can
make only a very small contribution

00:04:37.475 --> 00:04:43.355
to language acquisition, because
there are so many rules of grammar.

00:04:43.505 --> 00:04:46.505
And so few people who know all the rules.

00:04:46.775 --> 00:04:51.985
The idea that the learner will know
the rule when he or she needs to

00:04:51.985 --> 00:04:54.555
use it, will remember the rule.

00:04:55.305 --> 00:04:59.235
If the learner spends their time
and effort on thinking about the

00:04:59.235 --> 00:05:01.965
rule and I"'m about to say this
now, let's think about the rule.

00:05:01.965 --> 00:05:02.715
Is this correct?"

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.325
They won't be able to speak
because it's just simply too

00:05:05.325 --> 00:05:08.085
difficult to do while speaking.

00:05:08.655 --> 00:05:13.665
So the monitor, if anything,
can actually inhibit speaking.

00:05:14.745 --> 00:05:21.360
So the monitor, uh, as a means of,
you know, improving our knowledge

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.160
of a language is not very effective.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:23.730
Okay.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.950
The next thing is the input
or comprehension hypothesis.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.000
We, we learn languages when we
understand the message, when we're

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.560
interested in a message and we
understand the message comprehensible

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.000
input is the only thing that works
says Krashen in language acquisition.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.890
We have tried grammar rules.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.180
We have tried repetition drills and
the input hypothesis, uh, claims,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:55.210
however, that comprehending messages
is the only way language is acquired.

00:05:55.719 --> 00:05:59.650
There is no individual variation
in the fundamental process

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:00.969
of language acquisition.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:08.549
And then he goes on to explain
that we acquire language to input

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.080
when we already have enough things
that we have already acquired.

00:06:13.349 --> 00:06:18.000
So that I plus one, we, we acquired
that next thing that we could learn.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.510
We can learn once we have acquired
sort of the previous things.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.969
And so we learned from context.

00:06:25.034 --> 00:06:28.424
And therefore the teaching method
has to be focused on giving

00:06:28.604 --> 00:06:31.215
learners comprehensible input.

00:06:31.935 --> 00:06:36.344
Now a corollary of the, uh,
input hypothesis is that

00:06:36.344 --> 00:06:38.354
talking is not practicing.

00:06:38.474 --> 00:06:43.365
In other words, you can't output
your way to language acquisition.

00:06:43.905 --> 00:06:47.835
It is the input that's going to
give you the language acquisition.

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:53.095
If you get enough input, then you
will have that situation, sort of N+1,

00:06:53.115 --> 00:06:57.135
because we'll be getting so much input
that there will be elements there that

00:06:57.135 --> 00:07:00.975
you are already familiar with that
will enable you then to learn some

00:07:00.975 --> 00:07:04.395
additional elements with enough input.

00:07:06.075 --> 00:07:08.565
Now, immediately, people are going
to say, well, when you first start,

00:07:08.565 --> 00:07:09.705
you don't understand anything.

00:07:10.215 --> 00:07:16.155
And so Krashen in this book explains
how in a classroom, this sort

00:07:16.155 --> 00:07:18.585
of total physical response TPR.

00:07:19.275 --> 00:07:24.825
It's a method in the classroom where
the teacher can simplify the language

00:07:24.855 --> 00:07:30.915
content, can use gestures, can use
pictures, can use a whole range of

00:07:30.915 --> 00:07:35.745
things to help the learner start to
get a toehold in the language, create

00:07:35.745 --> 00:07:41.685
that N or he refers to it as I, so that
incrementally additional things can

00:07:41.685 --> 00:07:43.515
then be added to what is understood.

00:07:44.205 --> 00:07:48.450
I happen to believe, with a system
like LingQ, and people are going to

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:53.790
criticize me, but the idea that we can
create that same sheltered environment,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:59.340
even for people who are not in a
classroom, uh, using say the mini stories

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.450
where there's a lot of repetition of
vocabulary, you can look words up, you

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.310
can listen again, you can read again.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.159
Somewhere, you have to get
a start in the language.

00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:09.179
You have to get a start.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.460
Initially, nothing is comprehensible.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:15.750
You have to start to acquire little
bits and pieces so that you can then

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:19.710
build on that with additional things
that start to become comprehensible.

00:08:19.859 --> 00:08:23.400
And so the complexity of what you're
listening to, it gets greater and greater

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.000
as you're able to expand into new domains.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.590
I think I'll stop it there.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.809
He has a lot of research
to back up what he said.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.130
But I, I just think it's it's well
worthwhile anyone who's going to spend

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:39.659
the time and effort to learn a language
should understand how the process works.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.689
And there is no better
explainer than Stephen Krashen.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:48.000
So whether it be this particular
book or whether you simply go to

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.910
his website and download all of the
free material that's there, I'll

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:55.590
leave a link in the description
box, I very much suggest you do it.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:01.290
And, uh, I think armed with this
sense of how we learn, like, even

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:05.439
if we think we're not progressing,
it's obvious from what Krashen writes

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:09.300
based on all of his research and
experience, as long as we continue to

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:14.280
expose ourselves to the language, we
will continue to gradually improve.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:15.240
Okay.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.689
I thought I'd just bring you up to
date on, on where you can get some

00:09:18.689 --> 00:09:21.540
more information about Stephen Krashen,
because there were questions about that.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:22.290
Thank you.

